                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_read:          `<strong>Good — you read before you reached for a fix.</strong> Naming what Tom actually wants, what Aisha actually fears, and what your team is watching for is where emotional intelligence begins. A premature solution would have destroyed exactly that information.`,
                    coach_r1_decide:        `<strong>You've benched Tom in four minutes.</strong> You haven't named what Tom wants, what Aisha fears, or what your team is watching for. You've solved a logistics problem, not a people problem. Re-run it — tell me each person's real interest <em>before</em> you tell me your choice.`,
                    coach_r1_numbers:       `<strong>The numbers feel decisive, but they're the comfortable answer.</strong> A 31% reject rate and a £95k credit don't tell you what nine years of mentoring buys you, or what three watching engineers will conclude. Read the people first.`,
                    coach_r1_escalate:      `<strong>Handing both straight to HR abdicates the call you were promoted to make.</strong> The team is watching whether their new lead owns hard decisions or escapes them. You can use HR — but you cannot outsource being the manager.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp.</strong> You read the people first AND named the real clash: keeping Aisha's secret to help Tom means hiding the truth from the very people who must plan. That is Candour vs Care vs Courage in one sentence — now you can reason about the right thing.`,
                    coach_r1_notclash:      `<strong>This is not 'just resourcing'.</strong> If it were, you wouldn't feel the knot. The clash is real: helping Tom by leaning on Aisha rests on a fact you've been asked to conceal from HR and the owner. Name the values, or you'll optimise the wrong problem.`,
                    coach_r1_tomonly:       `<strong>You've collapsed it to the Tom question.</strong> But the Tom fix depends on Aisha — and Aisha's secret pits Candour against Care. Frame both, together, or you'll solve one and detonate the other.`,
                    coach_r1_loyalty:       `<strong>Careful — if the frame is 'my career', the decision is already compromised.</strong> The skeptical peer's narrative is noise; the real conflict is between honouring Aisha's confidence and the firm's right to plan. Lead from the values, not the optics.`,
                    coach_r1_independent:   `<strong>You're treating Tom and Aisha as two separate cases.</strong> But your Tom solution depends entirely on Aisha — who may be gone by Friday. You've built your fix on a person you privately believe is leaving. Decide them together, or watch both unravel at once.`,
                    coach_r2_full:          `<strong>A structured method gives the EI read a spine.</strong> Stakeholders, colliding duties, real options and a publicity test turn a felt reaction into a reasoned, defensible decision — exactly what a first-time manager needs when the clock is loud.`,
                    coach_r2_gut:           `<strong>'I know these people' is how confident managers walk into avoidable harm.</strong> Your gut is data, not a method. Under a Thursday deadline and a watching team, a disciplined sequence is faster than re-doing a decision that blows up.`,
                    coach_r2_binary:        `<strong>Keep-or-replace at the cheapest price is not ethical reasoning.</strong> It skips the stakeholders, the colliding duties and the publicity test — and it's exactly how you optimise the £95k credit while ignoring the £600k you can't see.`,
                    coach_r2_thirdway:      `<strong>There it is — the third option.</strong> You can honour Aisha's confidence about the offer itself <em>and</em> tell HR and the owner, truthfully, that the critical module needs a backup plan. That's the line between confidence and complicity: no lie, no breach.`,
                    coach_r2_conceal:       `<strong>You promised Aisha confidentiality, and now you're staffing her onto the only client-critical module while hiding from your owner that she may resign on Friday.</strong> When the milestone slips and they learn you knew — what happens to your credibility, and to the 'Candour' value on your own offer letter? <em>Was there a third option?</em>`,
                    coach_r2_expose:        `<strong>Telling HR exactly what Aisha confided betrays the confidence she trusted you with.</strong> You're honest to the firm but you've shown the team their new lead will repeat a private conversation. There was a route that protected the plan <em>and</em> the person — you skipped it.`,
                    coach_r2_fewoptions:    `<strong>One or two options is just keep-or-replace in disguise.</strong> If you didn't generate genuine alternatives, you didn't really choose. Force at least three per dilemma before you stress-test them.`,
                    coach_r2_lowpublicity:  `<strong>If you couldn't defend this to all 14 in the room, that's the tell.</strong> A decision that needs to stay hidden is usually the wrong one. Raise it to a standard you could say out loud to the whole team.`,
                    coach_r3_caretruth:     `<strong>That's the craft.</strong> The same true thing — the 31% reject rate, three missed sprints — delivered privately, owning that the system covered for him, with a real path forward, leaves Tom's dignity intact. Candour and care are not opposites; the delivery is the difference.`,
                    coach_r3_blunt:         `<strong>'Your reject rate is 31%, you're off the module' — true, and devastating to a mentor with nine years in.</strong> He'll be right about the facts and certain he was treated without care — and he'll tell everyone. Three long-tenured engineers just watched. That's how a £95k credit becomes £600k in attrition.`,
                    coach_r3_avoid:         `<strong>Quietly reassigning Tom with no honest conversation isn't kindness — it's cowardice dressed as care.</strong> He'll know. The team will know. You've breached Candour to dodge a hard hour, and taught fourteen people that feedback here is never straight.`,
                    coach_r3_ownerblind:    `<strong>Keeping the project owner in the dark is the complicity trap again.</strong> The owner needs the planning truth to protect Thursday. Hide it and you own the slip personally — and the 'Candour' value becomes a poster nobody believes.`,
                    coach_r3_dumpall:       `<strong>One mass email — including Aisha's situation — breaks the confidence and humiliates Tom in public.</strong> Disclosure is a sequence, not a broadcast. Who needs to know what, in what order, with whose dignity protected?`,
                    coach_r3_lowempathy:    `<strong>Walking in irritated at Tom and anxious about Thursday, unregulated, will leak into the room.</strong> Self-regulation is the EI muscle that lets you say the hard thing without making it about you. Read their fear before you manage your own.`,
                    coach_r3_nobackup:      `<strong>Your critical module now has no real backup owner.</strong> You've built the Thursday fix on Aisha — a person you privately expect to resign on Friday. If she goes, the module has no owner and the milestone is gone. Name a backup and ready it now.`,
                    coach_r4_eiethics:      `<strong>That's a board-ready defence.</strong> You read the people, ran the method, owned the trade-off, AND protected Thursday — and you can show the reasoning. That answers the skeptical peer far better than either a spreadsheet or a charm offensive.`,
                    coach_r4_numbers:       `<strong>You replaced Tom to protect a £95,000 service credit. Defensible.</strong> But three long-tenured engineers just watched you discard a nine-year colleague the way the last manager never would have. Trade a £95k credit for £600k in attrition and you optimised the number due Thursday, not the one due all year.`,
                    coach_r4_popularity:    `<strong>Keeping everyone happy is not leadership — it's avoidance.</strong> You kept Tom, kept the secret, dodged every hard conversation. Thursday is now at risk, Candour is breached, and the team has learned their new lead won't make the hard call. That confirms 'a stretch'.`,
                    coach_r4_authority:     `<strong>'The boss decides and the team falls in line' is pure authority — and it breaks the psychological contract.</strong> A peer just became a boss; legitimacy comes from fairness and candour, not position. You'll get compliance and lose the room.`,
                    coach_r4_noprecedent:   `<strong>There's no such thing as a one-off call.</strong> Your team's real question isn't 'what did she decide about Tom?' — it's 'what kind of manager is she?' Every dilemma decision is an act of culture-setting. Name the precedent, because they already read one.`,
                    coach_r4_strong:        `<strong>This is the manager the team needed to see.</strong> A defended, EI-led decision, an owned trade-off, a protected deadline, and a concrete first-30-days commitment that bends the 24% attrition down. You've turned a stretch promotion into legitimate authority.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>'My door is always open' is a slogan, not a commitment.</strong> The team has heard it from managers who then changed nothing. Name the specific behaviours — weekly 1:1s, transparent staffing, a fair process — or the attrition figure keeps climbing.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>'Survive Thursday, think about the team later' is exactly how the 24% figure was built.</strong> The first 30 days are when fourteen people decide whether to stay. No commitment is itself a precedent — and a bad one.`,

